\par
\b THE WINDOW\b0\par
\tab Module (top left): chooses the module. Each module has its own answers.\par
\tab Books: n of m... chooses the books to survey (see "Choosing the books" below).\par
\tab Rescan reads the books again and surveys again. Use it after changing the text. When the text changes in Paratext while the window is open, the status line reminds you to rescan.\par
\tab On the left, the module's questions, grouped by the sections of the Word document. The mark before each question shows its state: an empty circle = no answer yet; an orange diamond = suggested by the survey, not reviewed yet; a green dot = reviewed by the team.\par
\tab Topic and NIV (top right): the question and what the NIV does, as in the Word document.\par
\tab Our project (yellow): your answer. A suggestion from the survey has one sentence per line.\par
\tab Found in this project: what the survey found. The list on the left gives the patterns found and how many times; click one to see its examples on the right. "Your text at the NIV examples" shows the project's text at the verses the module gives as examples. Double-click an example to go to that verse in Paratext.\par
\par
\b ANSWERING\b0\par
1. Click a question on the left.\par
2. Read the suggestion in the yellow box and the findings below it. The survey counts what the text does; it cannot know what the team intends. Where the text is not consistent, the suggestion gives the numbers so the team can decide.\par
3. Correct or complete the answer, or type your own. Typing in the box, or ticking Reviewed, marks the answer as reviewed (green).\par
4. Use survey text puts the survey's suggestion back in the box.\par
Answers are saved when you move to another question, when you click Save and when you close the window. When the survey runs again (Rescan, a different choice of books), answers that are still only suggestions are replaced by the new suggestions; reviewed answers are never changed.\par
\par
\b CHOOSING THE BOOKS\b0\par
A project may have all 66 books while the style guide should describe only the New Testament, or the books drafted so far. Click Books: n of m... and tick the books to survey. The buttons on the right tick a group at once: All, None, Bible books, Old Testament, New Testament, Deuterocanon, Other books. Click OK.\par
The choice is the same for every module and is kept for the next time. The books are read again and the module is surveyed again. Books that are not ticked are not read at all, so the survey is also faster. The status line then says, for example, "27 of 66 books read (your choice)".\par
Some questions look at particular places, such as the lists in Genesis or the end of each Book of Psalms. When such a place is in a book that was not chosen, the findings say "book not surveyed" instead of "not drafted".\par
\par
\b EXPORTING TO WORD\b0\par
Click Export to Word... to save a copy of the module's Word document with the team's answers in the yellow "Our Project" column, one paragraph per line. If some answers are still unreviewed suggestions, the plugin asks whether to include them: Yes includes them, No leaves those cells empty. The file name offered is "<language> <module> Module (<project>).docx", in your Documents folder. After saving, the plugin offers to open the file.\par
The rest of the document (introduction, NIV column, formatting) is Biblica's original. Text that the form already had in a yellow cell (for example "Yes or No: ____") is kept, with the answer after it.\par
\par
\b WHERE THE ANSWERS ARE KEPT\b0\par
The answers are saved in the project folder, in local\\StyleGuide\\<module>.xml (for example local\\StyleGuide\\Punctuation.xml), and the choice of books in local\\StyleGuide\\settings.xml. Send/Receive does not share the local folder, so each computer has its own answers. To share the team's answers, export the Word document and send it.\par
\par
\b WHAT THE SURVEY LOOKS AT\b0\par
\tab Punctuation: sentence-final marks, opening \'bf \'a1, commas (including the serial comma), semicolons, colons, dashes, parentheses, brackets, ellipses, slashes, abbreviations and spacing before punctuation.\par
\tab Footnotes: kinds of footnotes (variants, literal meaning, wordplay...), the doubtful verses, the parts of each note (\\fr \\ft \\fq \\fqa) and where the callers are placed.\par
\tab Headings: book names (\\toc1-3, \\h, \\mt, BookNames.xml), section headings (length, capitalization, punctuation), introductions and outlines.\par
\tab Hyphenation: the project's list of hyphenated words (hyphenatedWords.txt) and the patterns of the words the team has approved.\par
\tab Lists: list paragraphs (\\li, \\lh, \\lf...), the places where the NIV uses a list, item punctuation, tables and ancient measures.\par
\tab Numbers: the number settings, numbers in digits or in words in each context, ordinals, times and measures.\par
\tab Poetry: poetic lines (\\q1 \\q2...), stanzas and \\b, lead-in lines, Psalms (titles, Books of Psalms, acrostics) and Proverbs.\par
\tab Quotations: the quotation marks of each level, continuers in later paragraphs, and quotation dashes.\par
\tab Syntax: letters and diacritics, words written in two ways, capitalization, apostrophes and questions.\par
\tab The draft modules: renderings of names and key terms from the project's Biblical Terms, and counts of markers and verse bridges.\par
For Spanish, French and Portuguese projects (by the language code in the project settings), the survey also recognizes number words, conjunctions and the wording of footnotes. For other languages it relies on the marks and markers.\par
